EXEC_SUMMARY = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "Executive Summary"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "This paper documents an unsupervised-learning analysis that organizes the remaining high-level radioactive tank waste at the Hanford Site into data-driven families, and then asks a single practical question for each current Savannah River Site (SRS) waste stream: does anything like this already exist at Hanford, and how close is the nearest match? The model was built from 433 Hanford tank-by-phase compositions in the Best-Basis Inventory (BBI), expressed in three separate feature spaces: 25 sludge-deposition elements, 15 salt-cage anions and cations, and 41 radionuclides. No labels, no site tags, and no processing history were supplied to the algorithm; the structure was learned from composition alone using Ward hierarchical clustering, with KMeans, HDBSCAN, and UMAP used for model selection, anomaly flagging, and visualization. Seventeen measured SRS samples from Savannah River National Laboratory (SRNL) qualification reports (sludge batches, post-retrieval filter follows, and Salt Waste Processing Facility salt batches) plus 51 SRS closure-residual vectors were then projected into the Hanford-fitted taxonomy."}, 
    {"t": "p", "text": "The screening outcome is concrete. Every projected SRS current-waste stream lands near a real Hanford composition, and the nearest-analog distances span 1.4 to 11.4 in shared-support Aitchison distance, a scale where 1 to 3 is typical of two Hanford tanks from the same cluster. In radionuclide space, SWPF salt batches 10 through 13 find Hanford supernatant analogs at distances of 2.6 to 3.4 (with 3 to 9 shared nuclides), comfortably inside Hanford's own same-cluster neighbor spread (median 3.1, 90th percentile 8.9): these are genuine compositional analogs. Salt batches 8 and 9 sit farther out (5.4 to 6.7, but with 19 shared nuclides supporting the comparison), and post-retrieval filter-follow sludges (PRFT batches 15, 22, 26) have no real Hanford analog in element space, driven by monosodium titanate at 12,200 to 16,225 micrograms per gram of dried solids, an engineered sorbent that no Hanford tank contains. Tank 40 Sludge Batch 10 slurry cannot be projected into the element space at all because its report omits every one of the 25 panel elements."}, 
    {"t": "p", "text": "The model quality numbers are deliberately modest and should shape how the results are used. Best silhouette scores run 0.19 to 0.29 across the three panels, Ward-versus-KMeans adjusted Rand indices run 0.71 to 0.79, and cophenetic correlations run 0.59 to 0.68. In other words: cluster boundaries are fuzzy, the hierarchy is only moderately tree-like, and individual assignments should never be treated as ground truth. The taxonomy's value is as a screening and ranking instrument: it turns the question 'is there a Hanford analog?' into a ranked list with explicit distances, support sizes, and cluster contexts, which a subject-matter expert can then verify analytically. It is a hypothesis generator and a coverage map, not an oracle."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "One result doubles as a data-basis audit. All 51 SRS closure-residual compositions, the conservative per-tank source terms decayed to a 2032 horizon that SRS publishes, land at or beyond the 95th percentile of their assigned Hanford clusters in both the element and radionuclide panels (and cannot be projected into the salt panel at all, because the closure basis does not report the salt anion suite). That is a quantitative confirmation of what the companion white paper argued qualitatively: closure residuals are compositionally unlike current tank contents, and mixing the two bases will corrupt any cross-site comparison. The unsupervised model detects the basis difference on its own, with no site labels, which is exactly the behavior one wants from an audit instrument."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Section 2 presents the taxonomy and all cross-site projections. Section 3 explains the mathematics (compositional data, the centered log-ratio transform, the Aitchison geometry, and the neutral-point treatment of below-detection values) in enough depth to interpret the distances. Section 4 details the full method. Section 5 converts the results into usage guidance: how to read a projection, when to trust an analog, and how to run the same screen for future SRS batches. Section 6 is a caveat ledger. The accompanying dashboard (dashboard/unsupervised_app.py) exposes every number in this paper interactively."},
]

INTRO = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "1. Introduction"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "1.1 The analog question"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Hanford has spent two decades characterizing its 177 tanks: the Best-Basis Inventory assigns every tank a current, best-estimate composition by waste phase, and the site's process history (bismuth phosphate, REDOX, PUREX, and the scavenging and washing campaigns that followed) is documented tank by tank. SRS has 51 tanks, a shorter and more homogeneous reprocessing history, and a current-waste characterization program that reports measured compositions only for processing batches: sludge batches qualified for the Defense Waste Processing Facility, post-retrieval filter follows, and salt batches qualified for the Salt Waste Processing Facility. When SRS engineers ask 'has anyone processed waste like this?', the natural reference library is Hanford's, and until now the lookup had to be done by hand, from memory, or not at all."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "This paper builds that lookup as an explicit statistical object. The idea is to let the compositions organize themselves into families (a taxonomy), to place every Hanford tank-phase in that taxonomy, and then to drop each SRS stream into the same space and report: which family it joins, how far it sits from that family's center, whether its distance is unusual by Hanford standards, and which specific Hanford tank-phases are its nearest neighbors. Nothing in the pipeline knows which site a composition came from during taxonomy construction; SRS material only enters afterward as transformed coordinates. The structure is therefore Hanford's own, and every SRS placement is a measurement against it."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "1.2 Why unsupervised"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "No training labels exist for the question being asked. Nobody has published a curated set of 'equivalent waste streams' across the two sites, and any such set would embed someone's judgment about what equivalence means. Unsupervised learning sidesteps the problem: the model finds structure in the Hanford data alone, and equivalence is then measured as distance in a defined geometry rather than asserted. This also keeps the audit property: if the model had access to site labels, its clusters could quietly encode the labels rather than chemistry. The price is interpretive care, and Section 5 is devoted to it: an unsupervised placement is evidence about compositional similarity, not about processability, safety, or treatability, all of which depend on properties (rheology, organic content, heat load per unit volume, particle size) that no public dataset carries at tank resolution."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The analysis is a companion to the site-comparison white paper released with the same database, which compared total inventories, phase splits, and activity densities. Where that paper asked 'how much waste does each site hold', this one asks 'what kinds of waste exist, and where does each SRS stream sit among them'. The two questions share the assembled database (56,700 rows across nine tables, including 46,600 Hanford BBI rows and 607 measured SRS sample rows) but almost nothing else: the present work is per-composition, multi-space, and geometric."},
]

