    {"t": "p", "text": "HDBSCAN's outlier scoring (GLOSH) behaves differently in the three panels, and the difference is instructive. In the radionuclide panel, exactly one Hanford point exceeds the 0.85 flag threshold: AW-104 sludge solid (score 0.850), followed by TX-105 (0.835), AZ-102 supernatant (0.832), T-111 (0.821), and C-103 (0.820). These are plausible candidates for compositional peculiarity (AW-104's radionuclide vector is sparse and Pu-tilted relative to its cluster), and they are the right starting points for a data-quality or history review. In the element panels, however, GLOSH flags 87 of 158 sludge points and 138 of 258 salt points at the same threshold. That is not 55 percent of Hanford being anomalous; it is a known artifact of GLOSH in sparse, high-entropy data, where missing-feature uncertainty inflates local density estimates. The correct interpretation is asymmetric: a high GLOSH score in the radionuclide panel is evidence of anomaly; a high score in an element panel is only a prompt to inspect the point. Section 4.3 explains the mechanism; the dashboard exposes the scores either way."},
